= Anchoring effect — Technical =

== Effect representation ==
For judgments J under low and high anchors A_L and A_H:

<pre>
E[J | A_H] > E[J | A_L]
</pre>

is the characteristic directional anchoring pattern.

Effect magnitude depends on scale, knowledge, anchor relevance, task, and elicitation.

== Adjustment account ==
Anchor-and-adjustment models propose a starting value followed by insufficient correction.

Time pressure or reduced cognitive resources can strengthen some adjustment-based effects.

== Selective accessibility ==
Considering whether a target exceeds an anchor can activate anchor-consistent knowledge, shifting subsequent estimates.

This mechanism predicts content-sensitive effects beyond simple numeric carryover.

== Scale and pragmatic effects ==
Anchors can also communicate scale information or experimental expectations.

Controls are required before attributing every anchor effect to one cognitive mechanism.

== References ==
* Tversky & Kahneman (1974).
* Epley & Gilovich anchoring work.
* Mussweiler selective-accessibility research.
